%[text] %[text:anchor:T_dab0] # Estimate DAB Converter Parameters
%[text] This script sizes the power-stage circuit parameters of a dual-active-bridge (DAB) converter under single-phase-shift (SPS) modulation. You specify the voltage ranges, power levels, switching frequency and device output capacitance; the script returns the turns ratio, series inductance, magnetizing inductance, dead time, output capacitance and the leakage inductance. It verifies soft switching over the operating envelope and applies the results to the plant and controller blocks.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ov] ## Overview
%[text] The DAB transfers power by phase-shifting the primary full-bridge square wave ahead of the secondary. With turns ratio $n=N\_1 /N\_2${"editStyle":"visual"} (primary-to-secondary), the secondary voltage reflected to the primary is $nV\_2${"editStyle":"visual"} and the voltage conversion ratio is:
%[text] $M=\\frac{nV\_2 }{V\_1 }${"editStyle":"visual"}
%[text] Under SPS the average power transferred is (with $D=\\phi /\\pi${"editStyle":"visual"} the normalized outer phase shift, $\\phi${"editStyle":"visual"} in radians):
%[text] $P\\left(\\phi \\right)=\\frac{nV\_1 V\_2 }{2f\_{sw} L}D\\left(1-D\\right)$
%[text] Maximum power occurs at $D=0.5$ ( $\\phi =\\pi /2${"editStyle":"visual"} ). The design proceeds to:
%[text] 1. Match the turns ratio to the nominal voltages.
%[text] 2. Size the series inductance $L${"editStyle":"visual"} at the lowest $V\_1 V\_2${"editStyle":"visual"} product, held below critical by a margin.
%[text] 3. Compute the dead time and the minimum soft-switching power from the device output capacitance $C\_{oss}${"editStyle":"visual"} and $L${"editStyle":"visual"} .
%[text] 4. Size the magnetizing inductance to guarantee zero-voltage switching (ZVS) down to the specified minimum power.
%[text] 5. Split the inductance into an external inductor or transformer leakage.  \
%[text] The [Annexure](internal:H_annex) at the end gives the equations and definitions.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_rate] ## Specify Converter Rating
%[text] Set the voltage ranges, power levels and switching frequency of the converter.
DABSpec.minInputVoltage  = simscape.Value(700,"V");   % Minimum input (primary) voltage
DABSpec.maxInputVoltage  = simscape.Value(800,"V");   % Maximum input (primary) voltage
DABSpec.minOutputVoltage = simscape.Value(450,"V");   % Minimum output (secondary) voltage
DABSpec.maxOutputVoltage = simscape.Value(600,"V");   % Maximum output (secondary) voltage

DABSpec.ratedPower        = simscape.Value(10000,"W");   % Rated (maximum) power
DABSpec.minSoftSwitchPower = simscape.Value(7000,"W");   % Minimum power at which ZVS must hold
DABSpec.switchingFrequency = simscape.Value(100e3,"Hz"); % Switching frequency
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_dev] ## Specify Device and Modulation Parameters
%[text] The device output capacitance sets the ZVS dead time and the minimum soft-switching power. The peak-power headroom sizes the series inductance. The SPS peak power (at $\\phi =\\pi /2${"editStyle":"visual"} , lowest $V\_1 V\_2${"editStyle":"visual"} ) is then that multiple of rated power. Above 1, rated power is reached below the $\\pi /2${"editStyle":"visual"} ceiling.
DABSpec.mosfetOutputCapacitance = simscape.Value(200e-12,"F"); % Switch output capacitance (Coss)
DABSpec.peakPowerHeadroom  = 1.15;  % SPS peak power (at pi/2, lowest V1*V2) as a multiple of rated
DABSpec.magCurrentFraction  = 0.1;  % Magnetizing current cap as fraction of rated primary current
%[text] The dead time computed from the resonant leg transition ( $t\_{\\textrm{dead}} =\\pi \\sqrt{2C\_{oss} L}${"editStyle":"visual"} ) is clamped to a practical range: it cannot fall below the hardware floor set by the processor and gate driver, nor rise above a usable fraction of the switching period. A large dead time steals from the power-transfer window and distorts the waveform.
DABSpec.minimumAllowedDeadTime = simscape.Value(200e-9,"s"); % Hardware floor set by the processor and gate driver
DABSpec.maximumAllowedDeadTime = simscape.Value(0.08/DABSpec.switchingFrequency.value,"s"); % Ceiling, 5-10% of period
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_hold] ## Specify Output Capacitor Hold-Up
%[text] The output capacitor is sized at the minimum output voltage, where it supplies the full rated load current for a few switching cycles, and its voltage must not fall by more than a small fraction of that voltage.
DABSpec.outputDischargeCycles = 3;    % Cycles the output capacitor holds the load on its own
DABSpec.outputVoltageRippleFraction = 0.05; % Maximum output voltage sag during that discharge (fraction)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_para] ## Specify Parasitic Parameters
%[text] Parasitic resistances affect the device currents and the efficiency. Specify realistic values from component datasheets.
DABSpec.seriesInductorESR    = 0.01;  % Series inductor ESR (Ohm)
DABSpec.primaryWindingR      = 0.01;  % Transformer primary winding resistance (Ohm)
DABSpec.secondaryWindingR    = 0.01;  % Transformer secondary winding resistance (Ohm)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_lser] ## Specify Series Inductance
%[text] Select how the series inductance is built: the transformer leakage alone, or an external inductor with a small transformer leakage. The distribution factor is the primary share of the leakage.
DABSpec.seriesInductanceOption = "Use transformer leakage inductance"; % Series inductance option %[control:dropdown:d4b1]{"position":[34,70]}
DABSpec.leakageDistributionFactor = 0.8; % Primary share of the transformer leakage
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_est] ## Estimate DAB Converter Parameters
%[text] Compute the turns ratio, series inductance, dead time, minimum soft-switching power, magnetizing inductance, and the leakage split. `DisplayTableFlag` shows the main design table here; `DisplaySoftSwitchingTable` is kept off so the soft-switching summary is shown in its own [Soft-Switching Region](internal:H_ssr) section below.
[designTable, DABDesign] = designDABConverter(DABSpec, ... %[output:group:9a40a41c] %[output:55e0f8b4]
    "DisplayTableFlag", true, "DisplaySoftSwitchingTable", false); %[output:group:9a40a41c] %[output:55e0f8b4]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ptc] ## Verify Power Transfer Across the Voltage Range
%[text] In SPS each bridge runs a fixed 50% duty cycle (180 deg conduction). The phase shift $\\phi${"editStyle":"visual"} between the bridges is the control variable, spanning $-\\pi /2${"editStyle":"visual"} (full reverse power) to $+\\pi /2${"editStyle":"visual"} (full forward power). Plot the SPS power-transfer curve versus $\\phi${"editStyle":"visual"} at the minimum, nominal and maximum voltage combinations, with the rated and minimum power levels overlaid. Confirm that the required phase shift at rated power stays inside the maximum-power ceiling at $\\phi =\\pm \\pi /2${"editStyle":"visual"} . The minimum power must also remain resolvable.
plotDABPowerTransferCurve(DABDesign); %[output:2a1c2bdd]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ssr] ## Soft-Switching Region
%[text] When the leakage inductance of the transformer is used as a primary DAB inductor, the series inductance is a T network. Part of $L${"editStyle":"visual"} sits on the primary side of the magnetizing branch and the rest on the secondary side, and the two add up to $L${"editStyle":"visual"}. 
%[text] Each switching event has to meet three conditions:
%[text] - Direction: the current at the switching instant must flow the way that conducts the anti-parallel diode and discharges the capacitance charge.
%[text] - Amplitude: the leading bridge sees its commutation-loop voltage rise during the dead time, so its current must reach $2\\sqrt{V\_1 \\left(nV\_2 \\right)C\_{\\textrm{diff}} /L\_{\\textrm{res}} }${"editStyle":"visual"} .
%[text] - Charge: the lagging bridge only has to move the node charge inside the dead time, so its current must reach $2C\_{\\textrm{diff}} V/t\_{\\textrm{dead}}${"editStyle":"visual"} . \
%[text] Forward power makes the primary the leading bridge and reverse power makes the secondary lead, so all four bridge and direction cases are checked and the worst one is reported. Both currents grow with $\\left|D\\right|${"editStyle":"visual"} , so light load is the hard case.
computeDABSoftSwitchingLimit(DABDesign, "DisplayTableFlag", true); %[output:36b86ae6]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_zvs] ### Soft-Switching Curves
%[text] Plot the region over the duty-ratio and voltage conversion-ratio plane, one curve per bridge, once for each power direction. Each curve is the smallest duty ratio at which that bridge reaches the current it needs. Both bridges have to commutate, so the shaded area beyond the outer curve is where the pair soft switches together. Markers show each $V\_1${"editStyle":"visual"} and $V\_2${"editStyle":"visual"} combination at the duty ratio it needs for rated and for minimum power, so a marker outside the shaded area hard switches. The curves hold at any voltage level, because every condition scales with $V\_1${"editStyle":"visual"} at a fixed $M${"editStyle":"visual"} .
%[text] The magnetizing current divides in inverse proportion to the inductance split, so with most of $L${"editStyle":"visual"} on the primary side the secondary bridge receives most of $I\_m${"editStyle":"visual"} . That pushes the secondary curve towards $D=0${"editStyle":"visual"} and leaves the primary bridge as the one that decides the floor over most of the plane. Forward power makes the primary the leading bridge and reverse power makes the secondary lead, so the two planes are not mirror images.
plotDABZVSRegion(DABDesign, "PowerDirection", "forward"); %[output:0fdb65b7]
plotDABZVSRegion(DABDesign, "PowerDirection", "reverse"); %[output:0fdb65b8]
%%
%[text] ## Specify Controller Parameters
%[text] The DAB converter uses a constant-current / constant-voltage (CC-CV) controller. It runs in one mode at a time, selected by the CCCV input: in CV mode a voltage-loop PI acts on the voltage error, and in CC mode a current-loop PI acts on the current error. The two loops are independent - each produces the phase shift directly; they are not cascaded. Set the gains for each loop, the sample time, controller types and phase-shift limits below. The base and timing parameters (nominal voltage, nominal power, switching frequency and dead time) come from the power-stage design.
DABController.voltageLoopProportionalGain = 0.0075;    % Kpv - voltage-loop proportional gain
DABController.voltageLoopIntegralGain     = 600;      % Kiv - voltage-loop integral gain

DABController.currentLoopProportionalGain = 0.0008;    % Kp - current-loop proportional gain
DABController.currentLoopIntegralGain     = 600;    % Ki - current-loop integral gain

DABController.sampleTime    = 1e-7;   % Ts - discrete controller sample time (s)
DABController.phaseShiftMax = pi/2;   % Maximum phase shift (rad)
DABController.phaseShiftMin = -pi/2;  % Minimum phase shift (rad)
DABController.voltageLoopIntegralGain     = 600;      % Kiv - voltage-loop integral gain
DABController.gateVoltage = 15; % Gate voltage (V)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_annex] ## Annexure: Equations and Definitions
%[text] ### Turns Ratio and Conversion Ratio
%[text] The nominal voltages are the mid-range averages, and the turns ratio matches them:
%[text] $V\_{1,\\textrm{nom}} =\\frac{V\_{1,\\min } +V\_{1,\\max } }{2}\\;,\\quad V\_{2,\\textrm{nom}} =\\frac{V\_{2,\\min } +V\_{2,\\max } }{2}\\;,\\quad n=\\frac{V\_{1,\\textrm{nom}} }{V\_{2,\\textrm{nom}} }$
%[text] The conversion ratio measures how well the two bridge voltages match. Its spread over the voltage range says how far the design moves away from a match:
%[text] $M=\\frac{nV\_2 }{V\_1 }\\;,\\quad M\_{\\max } =\\frac{nV\_{2,\\max } }{V\_{1,\\min } }\\;,\\quad M\_{\\min } =\\frac{nV\_{2,\\min } }{V\_{1,\\max } }$
%[text] ### Power Transfer
%[text] Power follows the phase shift, normalized as $D=\\phi /\\pi${"editStyle":"visual"} over $\\left\\lbrack -0\\ldotp 5,0\\ldotp 5\\right\\rbrack${"editStyle":"visual"} , and peaks at $\\left|D\\right|=0\\ldotp 5${"editStyle":"visual"} :
%[text] $P=\\frac{nV\_1 V\_2 }{2f\_{sw} L}D\\left(1-\\left|D\\right|\\right)\\;,\\quad P\_{\\max } =\\frac{nV\_1 V\_2 }{8f\_{sw} L}$
%[text] Inverting it gives the duty ratio an operating point needs, taking the root below $0\\ldotp 5${"editStyle":"visual"} :
%[text] $\\left|D\\right|=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\sqrt{1-\\frac{8f\_{sw} LP}{nV\_1 V\_2 }}\\right)$
%[text] ### Series Inductance and Dead Time
%[text] Size $L${"editStyle":"visual"} so the power ceiling at the lowest $V\_1 V\_2${"editStyle":"visual"} product is a headroom multiple of rated power. Headroom above 1 leaves rated power reachable below the $\\left|D\\right|=0\\ldotp 5${"editStyle":"visual"} ceiling:
%[text] $L\_{\\textrm{peakRated}} =\\frac{nV\_{1,\\min } V\_{2,\\min } }{8f\_{sw} P\_{\\textrm{rated}} }\\;,\\quad L=\\frac{L\_{\\textrm{peakRated}} }{\\textrm{peakPowerHeadroom}}$
%[text] The leg node capacitance $2C\_{oss}${"editStyle":"visual"} resonates with $L${"editStyle":"visual"} , and turning on at the resonant peak gives the dead time:
%[text] $t\_{\\textrm{dead}} =\\pi \\sqrt{2C\_{oss} L}\\;,\\quad t\_{\\textrm{dead},\\min } =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\sqrt{2C\_{oss} L}$
%[text] ### Commutation Currents
%[text] The series current is piecewise linear, with a breakpoint at each bridge's switching instant. Both magnitudes depend on $\\left|D\\right|${"editStyle":"visual"} alone, so reversing the power leaves them unchanged:
%[text] $\\left|i\_{\\textrm{pri}} \\right|=\\frac{\\left|V\_1 -nV\_2 \\left(1-2\\left|D\\right|\\right)\\right|}{4f\_{sw} L}\\;,\\quad \\left|i\_{\\textrm{sec}} \\right|=\\frac{\\left|nV\_2 -V\_1 \\left(1-2\\left|D\\right|\\right)\\right|}{4f\_{sw} L}$
%[text] The peak series current is the larger of the two, and the magnetizing current adds to it:
%[text] $I\_{\\textrm{peak}} =\\max \\left(\\left|i\_{\\textrm{pri}} \\right|,\\left|i\_{\\textrm{sec}} \\right|\\right)\\;,\\quad I\_{m,\\textrm{pk}} =\\frac{V\_1 }{4f\_{sw} L\_m }$
%[text] ### Soft Switching
%[text] The T network splits $L${"editStyle":"visual"} across the magnetizing branch, and each bridge resonates against its own side plus what it sees through the branch:
%[text] $L\_a =hL\\;,\\quad L\_b =\\left(1-h\\right)L\\;,\\quad L\_{\\textrm{res},\\textrm{pri}} =L\_a +\\left(L\_m \\parallel L\_b \\right)\\;,\\quad L\_{\\textrm{res},\\textrm{sec}} =L\_b +\\left(L\_m \\parallel L\_a \\right)$
%[text] The magnetizing current divides in inverse proportion to those two, so each bridge gets the share set by the inductance on the far side. Secondary quantities are referred to the primary:
%[text] $\\textrm{share}\_{\\textrm{pri}} =\\frac{L\_b }{L}\\;,\\quad \\textrm{share}\_{\\textrm{sec}} =\\frac{L\_a }{L}\\;,\\quad C\_{\\textrm{diff},\\textrm{pri}} =C\_{oss,\\textrm{pri}} \\;,\\quad C\_{\\textrm{diff},\\textrm{sec}} =\\frac{C\_{oss,\\textrm{sec}} }{n^2 }$
%[text] Direction condition, the sign test on the current at the switching instant:
%[text] $M\>1:\\left|D\\right|\>\\frac{M-1}{2M}\\;,\\quad M\<1:\\left|D\\right|\>\\frac{1-M}{2}$
%[text] Amplitude condition, for the leading bridge, whose commutation loop has to swing from one DC bus terminal to the other:
%[text] $\\left|i\\right|\\ge 2\\sqrt{\\frac{V\_1 \\left(nV\_2 \\right)C\_{\\textrm{diff}} }{L\_{\\textrm{res}} }}$
%[text] Charge condition, for the lagging bridge, which only has to move the node charge within the dead time:
%[text] $\\left|i\\right|\\ge \\frac{2C\_{\\textrm{diff}} V}{t\_{\\textrm{dead}} }$
%[text] The floor is the power at the smallest $\\left|D\\right|${"editStyle":"visual"} where both bridges meet all three conditions, taken over the four $V\_1${"editStyle":"visual"} and $V\_2${"editStyle":"visual"} combinations in both directions.
%[text] ### Magnetizing Inductance
%[text] Size $L\_m${"editStyle":"visual"} so $I\_{m,\\textrm{pk}}${"editStyle":"visual"} covers any shortfall in commutation current at the minimum power, capped so the circulating current stays a small share of the rated current:
%[text] $I\_{m,\\textrm{pk}} \\le \\textrm{magCurrentFraction}\\cdot \\frac{P\_{\\textrm{rated}} }{V\_{1,\\textrm{nom}} }$
%[text] ### Inductance Realization Split
%[text] The primary-referred total is always $L${"editStyle":"visual"} . With an external inductor, reserve $L\_{\\textrm{lk}} =\\beta L${"editStyle":"visual"} as transformer leakage, since the plant transformer needs positive leakage, and put the rest in $L\_r${"editStyle":"visual"} . With transformer leakage alone, $L\_{\\textrm{lk}} =L${"editStyle":"visual"} and $L\_r =0${"editStyle":"visual"} :
%[text] $L\_r =\\left(1-\\beta \\right)L\\;,\\quad L\_{\\textrm{lk1}} =hL\_{\\textrm{lk}} \\;,\\quad L\_{\\textrm{lk2}} =\\frac{\\left(1-h\\right)L\_{\\textrm{lk}} }{n^2 }\\;,\\quad L\_r +L\_{\\textrm{lk1}} +n^2 L\_{\\textrm{lk2}} =L$
%[text] The same $h${"editStyle":"visual"} gives the T-network split above: $L\_a =L\_r +L\_{\\textrm{lk1}}${"editStyle":"visual"} and $L\_b =n^2 L\_{\\textrm{lk2}}${"editStyle":"visual"} .
%[text] ### Output Capacitor
%[text] At the minimum output voltage the load current is $I\_{\\textrm{out}} =P\_{\\textrm{rated}} /V\_{2,\\min }${"editStyle":"visual"} . If it discharges the capacitor unreplenished for $N\_{\\textrm{cyc}}${"editStyle":"visual"} switching cycles, the output must sag by no more than a fraction $r${"editStyle":"visual"} of $V\_{2,\\min }${"editStyle":"visual"} :
%[text] $C\_o =\\frac{I\_{\\textrm{out}} \\left(N\_{\\textrm{cyc}} /f\_{sw} \\right)}{r\\,V\_{2,\\min } }=\\frac{P\_{\\textrm{rated}} N\_{\\textrm{cyc}} }{r\\,f\_{sw} V\_{2,\\min }^2 }$
%[text] ### Variable Definitions
%[text] $\\begin{array}{l}\nV\_1 ,V\_2 -\\textrm{Primary}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{secondary}\\;\\textrm{DC}\\;\\textrm{voltages}\\\\\nn=N\_1 /N\_2 -\\textrm{Transformer}\\;\\textrm{turns}\\;\\textrm{ratio}\\\\\nM-\\textrm{Voltage}\\;\\textrm{conversion}\\;\\textrm{ratio}\\\\\nL-\\textrm{Total}\\;\\textrm{primary}-\\textrm{referred}\\;\\textrm{series}\\;\\textrm{inductance}\\\\\nL\_a ,L\_b -\\textrm{Series}\\;\\textrm{inductance}\\;\\textrm{on}\\;\\textrm{the}\\;\\textrm{primary}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{secondary}\\;\\textrm{side}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;L\_m \\\\\nL\_{\\textrm{res}} -\\textrm{Commutation}-\\textrm{loop}\\;\\textrm{inductance}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;\\textrm{a}\\;\\textrm{bridge}\\\\\nL\_m -\\textrm{Magnetizing}\\;\\textrm{inductance}\\\\\nh,\\beta -\\textrm{Leakage}\\;\\textrm{distribution}\\;\\textrm{factor}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{leakage}\\;\\textrm{reserve}\\;\\textrm{fraction}\\\\\nf\_{sw} -\\textrm{Switching}\\;\\textrm{frequency}\\\\\n\\phi ,D-\\textrm{Outer}\\;\\textrm{phase}\\;\\textrm{shift}\\;\\left(\\textrm{rad}\\right)\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{its}\\;\\textrm{normalized}\\;\\textrm{value}\\;\\phi /\\pi \\\\\nC\_{oss} -\\textrm{Switch}\\;\\textrm{output}\\;\\textrm{capacitance}\\\\\nC\_{\\textrm{diff}} -\\textrm{Commutating}\\;\\textrm{capacitance}\\;\\textrm{of}\\;\\textrm{a}\\;\\textrm{bridge},\\;\\textrm{primary}-\\textrm{referred}\\\\\nt\_{\\textrm{dead}} -\\textrm{Dead}\\;\\textrm{time}\\\\\nI\_{m,\\textrm{pk}} -\\textrm{Peak}\\;\\textrm{magnetizing}\\;\\textrm{current}\\\\\nP\_{\\textrm{rated}} ,P\_{\\min } -\\textrm{Rated}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{minimum}\\;\\textrm{soft}-\\textrm{switching}\\;\\textrm{power}\\\\\nC\_o -\\textrm{Output}\\;\\textrm{capacitance}\\\\\nN\_{\\textrm{cyc}} ,r-\\textrm{Output}-\\textrm{capacitor}\\;\\textrm{discharge}\\;\\textrm{cycles}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;\\textrm{allowed}\\;\\textrm{output}-\\textrm{voltage}\\;\\textrm{sag}\\;\\textrm{fraction}\n\\end{array}${"editStyle":"visual"}
